<Project>

  <!--
    Centralized build settings. Values defined here apply to every project in
    the solution; individual .csproj files only override when they need to
    diverge. Keeps the "best-practice baseline" in one place.
  -->

  <PropertyGroup>
    <TargetFramework>net10.0</TargetFramework>
    <LangVersion>latest</LangVersion>
    <Nullable>enable</Nullable>
    <ImplicitUsings>enable</ImplicitUsings>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <PropertyGroup>
    <TreatWarningsAsErrors>true</TreatWarningsAsErrors>
    <EnforceCodeStyleInBuild>true</EnforceCodeStyleInBuild>
    <AnalysisLevel>latest</AnalysisLevel>
    <AnalysisMode>All</AnalysisMode>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!--
    Generate XML documentation by default. Test projects opt out by
    setting <GenerateDocumentationFile>false</GenerateDocumentationFile>
    in their csproj. (We can't gate this on $(IsPackable) here because
    the SDK locks in its decision before Directory.Build.targets runs.)
  -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <GenerateDocumentationFile>true</GenerateDocumentationFile>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!--
    Lock-file based version pinning. The lock file (packages.lock.json)
    captures exactly the resolved package versions and is committed to git,
    so any clone restores the same dependency graph. RestoreLockedMode fails
    the restore if the lock file is out of sync with the csproj.

    Unconditional, deliberately. This was gated on ContinuousIntegrationBuild,
    under a comment reading "only enabled in CI so local `dotnet add package`
    keeps working". This repository has no .github/workflows directory and no
    ref in its history ever contained one, so there was no CI for the gate to
    be enabled in: the sentence named a tradeoff between local convenience and
    a CI guarantee where only the convenience existed. The gate was not dormant
    pending a workflow, it was never on anywhere, and the comment implied
    otherwise. Nor does the SDK infer the property from a CI environment,
    measured on 10.0.400 in two sibling members: it evaluates empty even with
    CI and GITHUB_ACTIONS set.

    A file whose promise depends on a switch set somewhere else cannot state
    its own promise. Removing the condition makes the promise this file's own,
    which is the reason to prefer it over waiting for a workflow to carry it.

    What it buys here is more than it buys anywhere else in this project. This
    member consumes BigRational by PackageReference at 0.1.0-preview1, so the
    library's lock records it as "type": "Direct" with a requested range and a
    resolved version, which is exactly what locked mode compares. Every other
    member takes BigRational by ProjectReference, and locked mode does not
    compare a version arriving that way (halheinrich/Math#44), so their locks
    can go stale on that edge while restore still passes. Here they cannot,
    and the guarantee reaches both projects: the test project's lock carries
    the same constraint on its "type": "Project" edge to the library, because
    the edge underneath is a package one, so bumping the version fails the
    restore of both. That is measured, not inferred.

    That advantage is temporary and its expiry is scheduled. Migration step 7,
    folded into halheinrich/Math#10, converts this member to a
    ProjectReference; the BigRational edge stops being compared at that moment
    and this member inherits the exposure halheinrich/Math#44 describes, like
    the rest. Whoever makes that conversion is giving this up, and should know
    it is part of the price.

    Accepted cost: a deliberate package change now fails the restore with
    NU1004 instead of quietly rewriting the lock. Recover with
    `dotnet restore -p:RestoreForceEvaluate=true`, which is what the CLI's
    force-evaluate switch sets, and commit the regenerated lock. One command,
    once per intended change, on an artefact whose whole purpose is that it
    does not change silently. (The switch is spelled here as a property
    because an XML comment may not contain a double hyphen.) NuGet writes the
    regenerated lock with CRLF while .gitattributes pins it to LF, so
    `git status` then reports a change that `git diff` cannot show.
  -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <RestorePackagesWithLockFile>true</RestorePackagesWithLockFile>
    <RestoreLockedMode>true</RestoreLockedMode>
  </PropertyGroup>

</Project>
